  title="Named at Ekron"
  subtitle="A book titled after a devil, assigned to schoolchildren for seventy years."
  description="Lord of the Flies puts a blood sacrifice on a sharpened stake, dedicates it by name to a spirit, and has a boy sit down and take instruction from it. That is not symbolism laid over a plot. It is the plot."
>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>Lord of the Flies</strong> — William Golding, 1954. Filmed 1963 and 1990,
    adapted since. The novel governs all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>What It Shows</h2>
  <p>
    English schoolboys survive a crash on an empty island. No adults. Their order comes
    apart, and the fear on the island is given a name. They call it the beast.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hunters kill a sow. They cut off her head, sharpen a stick at both ends, drive
    one end into the ground, and jam the head onto the point. They leave it as an
    offering so the beast will let them alone. Flies settle and cover it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A boy named Simon comes on it alone and sits down in front of it. The head speaks to
    him. It calls itself the Lord of the Flies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blood sacrifice on a pole, dedicated by name to a spirit, followed by a human being
    taking instruction from the object. That is not symbolism laid over a plot. It
    <em>is</em> the plot.
  </p>

  <h2>What It Teaches</h2>
  <p>
    The head tells Simon the beast is not out in the jungle waiting to be hunted. It is
    inside them, part of them, and there is no killing it. Nothing in the book
    contradicts it. The story is built to prove it true.
  </p>
  <p>
    Simon carries the true report down the mountain and the crowd kills him with their
    hands. His death buys nothing. Nobody is cleared, nobody is changed, and another boy
    is murdered a few chapters later.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rescue finally arrives as a naval officer stepping onto the beach. A uniform, by
    chance. Not a redeemer, not a covenant.
  </p>

  <h2>What Scripture Says</h2>
  <p>
    Sin is a broken command, not a condition of the flesh (1 John 3:4). Where there is
    no law there is no transgression (Romans 4:15) — which is exactly why the island has
    no way out. Golding removed the courtroom in the first chapter and then spent the
    book proving nobody can leave the room.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the Day of Atonement the sin was carried away, and only after blood was shed
    (Leviticus 16:21-22; Hebrews 9:22). Carried away — not relocated into another boy,
    and not left sitting in the meat.
  </p>

  <h2>The Line</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>It's just a story about human nature.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    It is a story about a sacrifice on a stake that talks back, and it is named after a
    devil.
  </p>

